WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:12.480
Good evening.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:22.550
Welcome to Day Two of the World Science Festival
and also offer my congratulations to the nine

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:29.690
new Kavli Prize laureates that were announced
just this morning from Oslo with the satellite

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:33.440
feed to New York at the World Science Festival.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:41.359
And you know I'm not intimately familiar with
the work of those who won the prize in Nanoscience

00:00:41.359 --> 00:00:49.640
or Neuroscience, but I am deeply familiar
with the work that won the prize in Astrophysics.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:57.100
That work, as you may have read online already,
was the discovery, which was initially announced

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:01.659
in February, of the first direct detection
of gravitational waves.

00:01:01.659 --> 00:01:04.610
An absolutely wondrous discovery.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:12.070
Einstein predicted it a hundred years ago,
and now we have finally confirmed that.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:18.759
And I'll have occasion to come back to that
discovery for a brief description, probably

00:01:18.759 --> 00:01:26.700
a little bit later on, but suffice it to say
for now, that this discovery really feeds

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:36.229
into a pattern that we have been thrilled
to watch unfold over the course of a few hundred

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:44.509
years, where careful observation of the world,
careful mathematical analysis of the world

00:01:44.509 --> 00:01:51.110
has shown us that science has this amazing
capacity to give us deep insight into the

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:57.659
nature of the world, the nature of reality,
which is, which is really just spectacular.

00:01:57.659 --> 00:02:03.130
There's nobody who would deny that, except
maybe for the Republicans.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:04.130
But.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:07.200
No, no, no, no.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.780
But my point, my point.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:12.800
Ah stop that already.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:13.800
You know.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:20.420
No, my point is that this amazing progress
that's been made over the course of a few

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:32.330
hundred years has then emboldened scientists
to envision perhaps that all of the deep truths

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:37.600
of the world are susceptible to a scientific
explication.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:42.770
Some have gone out to say that in fact, the
only true knowledge about the world can be

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:47.080
gleaned through scientific analysis.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:52.500
Some have gone further still and describe
that everything that we hold dear, everything

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:59.100
that's important to us, everything that really
makes us human, is ultimately scientific at

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.110
its core.

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:07.250
In fact, there are some scientists who've
gone out, and I have to say typically it has

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:16.430
been physicists, who've gone out even in public
to make really scientific claims that this

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:19.900
sort of hegemony of science.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:21.930
Everything is ultimately describable by science.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:27.560
In fact, you know, perhaps you know one of
the most egregious examples of that occurred

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:28.560
just recently.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.270
Let me just show you a little clip of this.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:33.210
You and I are part of the expression of an
equation.

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:34.640
Yes, yes.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:38.520
That I would even say that without this holographic
idea.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:42.430
Math describes how your particles move, how
they behave how they interact.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:45.950
You are a bag of particles governed by the
laws of physics.

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:51.080
That is, that is a great pickup line.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:52.090
Brian Green.

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:54.110
Thank you so much.

00:03:54.110 --> 00:04:02.090
Now I should say, being on The Colbert Report
is not exactly the place where you can have

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:05.290
the most nuance come out of your perspective
of how the world works.

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:11.070
I hope this will be a forum where my actual
views on this will become a little bit more

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:12.070
clear.

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:17.440
And I've got a couple of great friends who
are going to join us in this conversation

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:21.699
to explore those kinds of ideas and many that
are related to them.

00:04:21.699 --> 00:04:25.480
So let me now bring them both on to the stage.

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:31.371
So first, Leon Wieseltier is the Isaiah Berlin
Senior Fellow in Culture and Policy at the

00:04:31.371 --> 00:04:32.570
Brookings Institution.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:35.540
He's the author among other books of the acclaimed
Kaddish.

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:40.720
He was a literary editor of The New Republic
from 1983 to 2014, and is now contributing

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:43.650
editor and critic at The Atlantic.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:55.130
Please welcome Leon Wieseltier.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:05:01.190
And we are also thrilled to be joined by Miguel
Nicolelis, who is the Duke School of Medicine

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:06.970
Distinguished Professor of Neuroscience, Professor
of Neurobiology, Biomedical Engineering and

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:12.720
Psychology and Neuroscience, founder of Duke
Center for Neuro-Engineering, founder and

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:16.900
scientific director of the Edmond and Lily
Safra International Institute for Neuroscience.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:23.150
He’s also founder of the Walk Again Project,
an international consortium of scientists

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:29.370
and engineers dedicated to the development
of an exoskeleton device to assist severely

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:36.930
paralyzed patients in regaining full body
mobility.

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:44.990
So thank you for joining us.

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:50.330
Some of the questions that we may discuss
here tonight, just to rattle off a few: are

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:56.350
we nothing but the physical processes that
make us up as that ridiculous person said

00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:57.470
in that clip.

00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:03.260
Is consciousness nothing but the physical
chemical and biological processes in the brain,

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:04.980
or is there more to it?

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:11.390
Is empirical knowledge at the core of everything
that we should believe as being true?

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:15.810
Are there human experiences that are simply
unsuited for scientific explanation?

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:21.790
Does the brain experience give us a misleading
view of reality?

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:24.560
What defines us and what defines a human difference?

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:31.340
So these kinds of questions, which really
span art, culture, religion, creativity, are

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:33.860
all fair game for us tonight.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:38.180
And let me just throw it to Leon to get us
going.

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:40.200
Thank you, my friend.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:50.889
I am a militant unreconstructed humanist who
has many fewer hours in my day than you clearly

00:06:50.889 --> 00:06:52.760
do.

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:57.860
I am, I confess, I am a little bit ashamed
at the level of my scientific knowledge but,

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:01.560
nonetheless some of the questions that I want
to talk about, that we will talk about tonight

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:04.750
are, are not just scientific questions.

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:09.240
They exceed the realms of science, or rather
the boundaries of the realm of science is

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.169
what we're going to be talking about.

00:07:11.169 --> 00:07:14.960
And what I thought I would do just to start
it off, just for a few minutes, is to just

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:21.199
state a few beliefs that I have and et cetera.

00:07:21.199 --> 00:07:26.430
So the first thing I want to say, the first
one, is just as a general matter that the

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:32.120
question of the place of science in life is
not a scientific question.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:34.169
It's a philosophical question.

00:07:34.169 --> 00:07:41.090
And no amount of scientific knowledge, no
amount of math, no amount of data, can tell

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:49.930
us where to put in our lives the experiments
and the math and the data.

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:53.960
That is something that is not a scientific
matter.

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:57.740
And one can, one gets into this interesting
discussion about what the word philosophy

00:07:57.740 --> 00:07:59.150
would mean.

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:00.150
But et cetera.

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:05.860
The second belief I have is that we live in
many realms.

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:12.580
The human existence is comprised of many realms,
and that the differences between these realms

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:13.780
are real.

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:17.800
That is to say the difference, and we can
define what these realms are in various ways,

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:22.300
but that they have their categories that are
appropriate to each, their methods of inquiry

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:24.080
that are appropriate to each.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:29.710
Their, the pace at which they're experienced
differ as well.

00:08:29.710 --> 00:08:36.800
And each of these realms may shed light on
each other so that it can be illuminating

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:41.899
to answer a political question with an observation
about culture, or an economic question with

00:08:41.899 --> 00:08:44.219
an observation about politics.

00:08:44.219 --> 00:08:51.509
But none of these realms, none of these, no
single, no one of these realms can, deserves

00:08:51.509 --> 00:08:53.970
to dominate all the others.

00:08:53.970 --> 00:09:00.189
And none of the realms can be reduced to any
one of them.

00:09:00.189 --> 00:09:07.300
And the attempt to reduce all the realms to
one is a kind of intellectual imperialism

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:11.829
and a colossal act of intellectual simplification.

00:09:11.829 --> 00:09:16.329
Now most scientists that I've met are not
remotely guilty of this imperialism.

00:09:16.329 --> 00:09:21.980
Practicing scientists, I must say that they
are one of the lessons that science, science

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:25.980
obviously doesn't just teach arrogance, it
also teaches humility.

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:30.680
And I've noticed that about my scientist friends
and so on.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:35.800
But there are cases, there are cases, in which
both the natural certain and certain of the

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:44.290
natural sciences and certain of the social
sciences have presumed, have presumed to the

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:47.819
driver's seat in our explanation of human
life.

00:09:47.819 --> 00:09:53.309
In the social sciences the most egregious
imperialist in my view is economics.

00:09:53.309 --> 00:09:58.290
We now live in a society in which some of
our most reputed authorities on the subject

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:02.540
of happiness are economists, which does not,
happiness I must say.

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:07.180
I know this is archaic-happiness does not
seem to me to be an economic subject.

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:12.059
But again, I know that's very old fashioned.

00:10:12.059 --> 00:10:18.380
We're living similarly in the in the era in
which we're living in which our culture certainly,

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:24.149
but also our intellectual life is increasingly
overwhelmed by data.

00:10:24.149 --> 00:10:26.850
So that everything is being quantified.

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:32.689
One of the imperialisms that we have to be
vigilant about is the quantification of things

00:10:32.689 --> 00:10:34.740
that cannot be quantified.

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:39.509
One of the questions that we have to ask as
a culture, is what can a number capture and

00:10:39.509 --> 00:10:42.050
what can't the number capture.

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:47.380
And a lot of the direction and the quality
of our culture as it develops, will I think

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:52.980
, will be owed to whether or not we learn
to resist the lure of the easy clarity and

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:58.300
the easy confidence that numbers can give
people about things that cannot be mathematically

00:10:58.300 --> 00:10:59.300
measured.

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:05.220
I remember once, and I used to work for a
magazine called The New Republic, and once

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:10.879
I was walking by where some young people who
work there and I overheard one young person

00:11:10.879 --> 00:11:15.459
say to the other, I'll never forget this,
he said, "Well my friend ran into her yesterday

00:11:15.459 --> 00:11:22.410
and she told him that she loves me, which
is a really important data point."

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:26.589
So I approached, you know I didn't want to
be obnoxious and I said you know, I promised

00:11:26.589 --> 00:11:31.670
not to pry and forgive me if you think I am,
I just need to point out to you that the fact

00:11:31.670 --> 00:11:34.470
that she loves you is not a data point.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:38.649
And if you'd like to discuss this, then he
came in and we discussed it and etcetera,

00:11:38.649 --> 00:11:39.649
etcetera.

00:11:39.649 --> 00:11:45.649
I think you actually said, "It's not a fucking
data point," if I remember the story correctly.

00:11:45.649 --> 00:11:51.379
You know I've got to say, Trump is everywhere.

00:11:51.379 --> 00:11:53.959
Just.

00:11:53.959 --> 00:11:56.050
But also, there are other examples one could
give.

00:11:56.050 --> 00:12:01.420
So for example, consider the physical or the
chemical analysis of a painting.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:02.820
Right?

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:03.820
It is possible.

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:07.149
You take a painting by Chardin, a painting
of cherries.

00:12:07.149 --> 00:12:12.470
It is possible for a chemist to cast all kinds
of light on how those chromatic effects are

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:18.209
accomplished with an analysis of pigments,
chemical analysis of pigments, and so on.

00:12:18.209 --> 00:12:25.660
But if the question that is being asked is,
"Why is that painting beautiful?", then the

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:30.070
chemical analysis of a painting is not going
to be able to answer that question.

00:12:30.070 --> 00:12:35.509
Even though as I say, there will be nothing
false about the chemical analysis of the painting

00:12:35.509 --> 00:12:40.779
because at some level, all those pigments
on Chardin's canvas are chemicals, et cetera

00:12:40.779 --> 00:12:41.779
et cetera.

00:12:41.779 --> 00:12:46.079
But could you imagine, sorry to jump in here,
but could you imagine at some point in the

00:12:46.079 --> 00:12:54.499
far future, and Miguel you should weigh in,
we will understand the brain with such exquisite

00:12:54.499 --> 00:13:01.540
precision and accuracy that the correlation
between the feeling of beauty, the choice

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:07.889
of colors that are being made by an aesthetic
judgment, we understand the physical processes

00:13:07.889 --> 00:13:13.879
so well that correlate with all of those things
that yes, we'll never say that they are the

00:13:13.879 --> 00:13:21.790
act of choice, they are the reason why Rembrandt
chose blue or red but we can say, it is just

00:13:21.790 --> 00:13:26.529
a physical process that we understand completely
well and if we could look into the painter's

00:13:26.529 --> 00:13:31.589
head, we would actually know that they are
choosing red at that moment and that's the

00:13:31.589 --> 00:13:34.170
physical correlate of that choice.

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:36.869
I think well- go ahead.

00:13:36.869 --> 00:13:38.389
As a neuroscientist, if I can get in.

00:13:38.389 --> 00:13:46.100
Well I mean
First of all, I like to thank for the invitation.

00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:50.749
This is a wonderful, is a wonderful opportunity
to debate these things and I think we're very

00:13:50.749 --> 00:13:57.489
similar in our approaches, because even though
I grew up as a scientist recording brainstorms,

00:13:57.489 --> 00:14:01.820
recording electrical brainstorms and trying
to look into thousands of neurons and find

00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:06.929
exactly what you're talking about, the answer,
the prediction of a behavior that is about

00:14:06.929 --> 00:14:08.139
to happen.

00:14:08.139 --> 00:14:10.480
So far you can do for some things.

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:15.139
You can do, for instance, for motor acts you
can reasonably, we can link these days brains

00:14:15.139 --> 00:14:21.259
to machines and can use just electrical activity
from human or animal brains to control directly

00:14:21.259 --> 00:14:23.670
the movements of a robotic device.

00:14:23.670 --> 00:14:29.399
Yet, when we go to exact the questions that
you were raising, I start wondering myself

00:14:29.399 --> 00:14:36.459
as a neuroscientist, not that I'm disputing
your point that you know the laws of physics,

00:14:36.459 --> 00:14:42.709
the brain has to obey the laws of physics,
but to some degree, certain attributes that

00:14:42.709 --> 00:14:48.839
came with the degree of complexity that evolution
endowed us may not be simply predicted by

00:14:48.839 --> 00:14:53.829
the same, you know, mechanisms that are used
to apply to understand the universe that is

00:14:53.829 --> 00:14:55.010
up there.

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:59.990
Because the universe that is between our ears,
I dare to say in front of a famous theoretical

00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:02.410
physicist, is much more complex.

00:15:02.410 --> 00:15:07.639
It's much more complex in many domains.

00:15:07.639 --> 00:15:13.439
A small footnote, I would say that I completely
agree with you and the reason why I do physics,

00:15:13.439 --> 00:15:15.369
is because it is so much simpler.

00:15:15.369 --> 00:15:17.670
And I mean that quite seriously.

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:23.199
We have been able in quantum mechanics to
understand the behaviors of systems with a

00:15:23.199 --> 00:15:26.910
few particles we can do that really well and
that's exciting.

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:31.199
But you give me something with all of the
connections and particles inside the head,

00:15:31.199 --> 00:15:33.329
there's no physicist alive who can do anything
with that.

00:15:33.329 --> 00:15:37.980
And I would just provoke you by saying that
all this beautiful, and I'm a big fan of quantum

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:42.300
mechanics and physics in general, although
I am of course just an amateur.

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:49.189
But the beauty of it is all that you guys
have done came from this, came from the biology

00:15:49.189 --> 00:15:50.809
that evolution produced.

00:15:50.809 --> 00:15:57.619
So I'd like to say, I mean like that then
what is out here, is the human universe.

00:15:57.619 --> 00:16:03.490
Because this is epic told by the human brain
is a reconstruction the best we can.

00:16:03.490 --> 00:16:08.249
Science is that, in my opinion like Niels
Bohr used to say, it's the best attempt we

00:16:08.249 --> 00:16:12.209
have that you know to describe what is out
there.

00:16:12.209 --> 00:16:18.809
It's the best shot that we have of reconstructing
this and is actually coming all these concepts;

00:16:18.809 --> 00:16:24.019
electrons, quarks, fields is all coming from
the biology of our brains.

00:16:24.019 --> 00:16:31.029
Wait, so are you saying that when we contact
aliens in the far, far future and we asked

00:16:31.029 --> 00:16:35.189
them for their view of how the universe is
put together, there's a chance that they're

00:16:35.189 --> 00:16:38.970
going to come out with something radically
different from anything that we're discovered

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:40.439
here cause their biology is different?

00:16:40.439 --> 00:16:42.009
We were talking about this backstage.

00:16:42.009 --> 00:16:47.170
I think if, I'm from the 60s so I'm Mr. Spock's
fan right.

00:16:47.170 --> 00:16:50.199
Growing up in Brazil, Mr. Spock was a hero.

00:16:50.199 --> 00:16:53.089
You're talking about the Star Trek Mr. Spock
Star Trek, yeah, yeah.

00:16:53.089 --> 00:16:54.720
-or the other guy? Dr. Spock.

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:57.459
I think they're both doctors, but anyway.

00:16:57.459 --> 00:16:59.889
But I would say exactly, that's my point.

00:16:59.889 --> 00:17:06.079
That if you go to Vulcan, in natural selection
evolution in Vulcan has been completely different

00:17:06.079 --> 00:17:10.329
and a complete different brain has evolved
with complete different properties.

00:17:10.329 --> 00:17:15.159
Mathematics, logic, science may be very different
the way they expressed these concepts and

00:17:15.159 --> 00:17:20.080
the way they reconstruct a universe and there
is a chance, I cannot deny that, that they

00:17:20.080 --> 00:17:25.470
may come up with an explanation that may have
some overlaps of ours, but may be completely

00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:26.570
different from what we are.

00:17:26.570 --> 00:17:29.740
And not isomorphic not just a different language.

00:17:29.740 --> 00:17:32.620
You're saying , you're saying literally different.

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:42.220
But I have to believe that, that if there
are Jews on other planets say, (laughter)

00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:50.549
that, that no matter how different their fates
will be, and I hope they're very different,

00:17:50.549 --> 00:17:57.760
that the, even if they come from different
biological origins, that each in their own

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:04.590
way by their own path can hit upon whatever
is actually true about the universe and that

00:18:04.590 --> 00:18:09.720
we do not need to condemn ourselves to the
idea that the different biology, do a kind

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:15.779
of biological cosmic relativism, whereby every
brain will have its own truth.

00:18:15.779 --> 00:18:19.070
I thought you were going to say they have
a guilt complex that comes from your mother

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:20.070
or something.

00:18:20.070 --> 00:18:21.070
No, no, no.

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:22.070
I said Jews on other planets.

00:18:22.070 --> 00:18:27.510
On that planet of yours, besides Jews there
are Brazilian's.

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:28.510
Yes.

00:18:28.510 --> 00:18:30.210
They'll come and say I doubt it.

00:18:30.210 --> 00:18:31.750
Let's try to do the experiment.

00:18:31.750 --> 00:18:35.650
It's a difficult experiment because you know,
they haven't arrived yet.

00:18:35.650 --> 00:18:40.480
But I think we should be open to this as I
think after what I heard from you.

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:44.900
I think neuroscience has to be open to all
the issues that you raised, because when we

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:50.530
talk about the brain, we tend to, as neuroscientists,
we tend to you know be away or try to be away

00:18:50.530 --> 00:18:51.620
from these big questions.

00:18:51.620 --> 00:18:54.230
And I think that it's time to, for us to open
up.

00:18:54.230 --> 00:18:55.230
Let me just say two things.

00:18:55.230 --> 00:18:59.830
I think first, there is a difference between
neuroscience and the reception and the use

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:02.440
of neuroscience in our culture.

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:07.669
And one of the things that I see happening
that so alarms me is the way in which more

00:19:07.669 --> 00:19:12.350
and more people are now sitting at the edge
of their chairs waiting for the latest bulletin

00:19:12.350 --> 00:19:17.390
from neuroscience so they can understand how
to proceed or understand their own lives.

00:19:17.390 --> 00:19:22.830
You know, so you get you get books that tell
you that Proust is a neuroscientist for example,

00:19:22.830 --> 00:19:26.429
to which my thought is immediately, well if
Proust is a neuroscientist and I've already

00:19:26.429 --> 00:19:29.850
read Proust, then I don't need neuroscience.

00:19:29.850 --> 00:19:31.659
Or that Jane Austen is a game theorist.

00:19:31.659 --> 00:19:35.559
We've had that too, similar response, similar
response.

00:19:35.559 --> 00:19:39.529
So I think that one of the things we have
to be aware of, there is the science, and

00:19:39.529 --> 00:19:43.640
then there's the uses to which the science
is put.

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:48.870
Generally, it's been my observation that people
do not like living with uncertainty.

00:19:48.870 --> 00:19:55.440
And the two, the two sources, the two most
reliable sources of certainty that they can

00:19:55.440 --> 00:19:58.090
find, are science and religion.

00:19:58.090 --> 00:20:03.480
Now the search for certainty in both of them
often betrays the spirit of both science and

00:20:03.480 --> 00:20:04.669
religion.

00:20:04.669 --> 00:20:10.759
But people need, they need to feel that in
some way their lives are an inevitable outcome

00:20:10.759 --> 00:20:11.759
of something.

00:20:11.759 --> 00:20:12.759
So are those different?

00:20:12.759 --> 00:20:16.259
I mean how-
Yeah I think they operate differently and

00:20:16.259 --> 00:20:22.549
I think that that's vulgar science and vulgar
religion because obviously doubt and skepticism

00:20:22.549 --> 00:20:24.970
is an important part of both.

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:28.870
But people don't always describe religion
in that way of course, right?

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:36.259
I mean that's a, probably a distinct view
from what most people envision as an environment

00:20:36.259 --> 00:20:44.299
in which faith is meant to guide your perspective
as opposed to some sort of skeptical, quizzical

00:20:44.299 --> 00:20:45.299
point of view.

00:20:45.299 --> 00:20:49.909
I think there is no, no I think, I mean this
is just a detour cause I wanted to make another

00:20:49.909 --> 00:20:56.250
point we're saying, but I think that there
is no religious tradition that doesn't include,

00:20:56.250 --> 00:21:01.400
not just in its depths but in any one of its
sophisticated levels, important elements of

00:21:01.400 --> 00:21:02.400
doubt, skepticism.

00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:06.250
In other words, it is I say it's a vulgar
idea of religion.

00:21:06.250 --> 00:21:10.480
The belief in God raises as many questions
as it answers except for people who don't

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:12.529
want questions raised.

00:21:12.529 --> 00:21:17.230
And people have a powerful interest, some
people, in not having questions raised.

00:21:17.230 --> 00:21:23.580
They want the matter settled and they look
for, as I say, for sources of certainty, for

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:28.970
sources of intellectual confidence about their
circumstances.

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:33.639
And they don't like the feeling that they
are burdened with the full responsibility

00:21:33.639 --> 00:21:36.370
of their circumstances etcetera, etcetera.

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:41.610
But the other thing I wanted to say when we
talked about the painter, about the painter,

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:46.940
it's very important to make a distinction,
I think, between causes and reasons.

00:21:46.940 --> 00:21:52.250
I think that an artist's, an artist's production,
a painting has both.

00:21:52.250 --> 00:21:55.450
It has causes in the sense that in some way
it was the product.

00:21:55.450 --> 00:22:00.299
I mean, you know the Divine Comedy was the
product of Dante’s brain, not just his mind.

00:22:00.299 --> 00:22:01.899
But reasons are something else.

00:22:01.899 --> 00:22:07.810
In other words, reasons are, the question
of why an artist made certain decisions both

00:22:07.810 --> 00:22:14.399
small decisions of execution and large decisions
of conception and interpretation.

00:22:14.399 --> 00:22:18.730
I don't believe that those decisions can be
explained only in terms of causes.

00:22:18.730 --> 00:22:20.270
I really don't.

00:22:20.270 --> 00:22:26.049
No, no, I'm with you because one of the things
I have been looking lately, because of this

00:22:26.049 --> 00:22:32.429
interest in larger scale brain activity and
how it may either determine or influence art

00:22:32.429 --> 00:22:39.230
and other aspects is that, there was a moment
in our history of art the people just painting

00:22:39.230 --> 00:22:44.100
like a photographer, reproducing form and
shaping very precise.

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:46.540
And that was how an artist was rewarded.

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:50.070
By basically reproducing what is out here
in great detail.

00:22:50.070 --> 00:22:54.509
And suddenly, in the beginning of the 19th
century, and that's the painting I brought

00:22:54.509 --> 00:22:58.850
from Turner you know, shape start being exploded.

00:22:58.850 --> 00:23:02.940
Things started exploding and the painters
just start saying, "I don't want to paint

00:23:02.940 --> 00:23:04.700
what is out there and everybody sees equal.

00:23:04.700 --> 00:23:09.230
I want to paint what I see, what comes from
me, from the inside.

00:23:09.230 --> 00:23:12.270
I want to representation of my brain's own
point of view."

00:23:12.270 --> 00:23:14.919
That's how I as a neuroscientist, I would
put.

00:23:14.919 --> 00:23:22.340
In light, the reflection of light, like what
we see here in this steamboat of Turner, was

00:23:22.340 --> 00:23:30.179
the basically the beginning of the Impressionist
movement in which shape suddenly starts to

00:23:30.179 --> 00:23:33.649
disappear, and in modern art it exploded.

00:23:33.649 --> 00:23:40.529
Funny enough, this explosion of space, this
disappearance of space, coincides with the

00:23:40.529 --> 00:23:44.960
investigation in physics of the very little
and the very big.

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:50.299
When you go back to the atomists, atomistic
view, you know, of nature at the end of 19th

00:23:50.299 --> 00:23:55.019
century, and you come with you know quantum
mechanics and general relativity any space

00:23:55.019 --> 00:24:01.070
in our conception explodes from the very tiny
to the huge to the whole universe.

00:24:01.070 --> 00:24:07.049
And I found it very beautiful that these analogies
or this his period shows this correlation

00:24:07.049 --> 00:24:09.010
in art and science.

00:24:09.010 --> 00:24:11.080
Where you are coming out of these boundaries.

00:24:11.080 --> 00:24:16.990
But I would say that if you look at that Turner,
that whereas it's certainly true that he was

00:24:16.990 --> 00:24:23.790
studying the difficulties and the dramas of
perception itself, I wouldn't read it entirely

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:27.870
as an allegory or an expression of his inner
states.

00:24:27.870 --> 00:24:32.960
I think that he would have said that that
painting was what it looked like.

00:24:32.960 --> 00:24:37.690
I don't think he would have said just that
it was what it looked like to me, even though

00:24:37.690 --> 00:24:42.940
he would have been the first to acknowledge
the subjectivity, the partiality, of his perspective.

00:24:42.940 --> 00:24:46.549
So I really believe, and the same with the
Impressionists, the same with all, they were

00:24:46.549 --> 00:24:52.020
making they were making a claim about the
difficulty of capturing perception the extent

00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:56.029
to which perception is involved in our understanding
of objectivity.

00:24:56.029 --> 00:25:00.639
But I don't think any of them were saying
this is just an expression of my brain.

00:25:00.639 --> 00:25:05.529
But what I was, I was trying to say is that
my conception of the brain right now after

00:25:05.529 --> 00:25:12.519
30 years is going from different models of
what we actually see as a result is this collision

00:25:12.519 --> 00:25:18.600
of what is out here in his being brain in
what is the brain's own point of view?

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:20.990
What is the brain is expecting as reality?

00:25:20.990 --> 00:25:22.899
And it's this collision.

00:25:22.899 --> 00:25:27.340
And I remember Picasso saying when a journalist
asked him in Paris what was that painting

00:25:27.340 --> 00:25:28.340
about.

00:25:28.340 --> 00:25:32.129
One of his Cubist paintings and he said, "Well
if I knew, I would not have painted it," you

00:25:32.129 --> 00:25:33.129
know?

00:25:33.129 --> 00:25:34.129
Yeah.

00:25:34.129 --> 00:25:37.669
Well you know there's this, you remind me
that this wonderful story about Matisse who

00:25:37.669 --> 00:25:42.080
near the end of his life was commissioned
to paint a fresco in a small church in Vence

00:25:42.080 --> 00:25:43.570
in the south of France.

00:25:43.570 --> 00:25:48.490
And a filmmaker asked for permission to film
him painting, and he granted permission and

00:25:48.490 --> 00:25:52.259
then when it was over the filmmaker showed
him the film, and Matisse was horrified and

00:25:52.259 --> 00:25:53.960
didn't want the film shown.

00:25:53.960 --> 00:25:57.649
And when the filmmaker asked why the artist
said, "Because your film makes it look like

00:25:57.649 --> 00:25:59.539
I knew what I was doing."

00:25:59.539 --> 00:26:05.639
I think you know there is, again I think that
this whole thing we're getting at in different

00:26:05.639 --> 00:26:10.769
ways about the, the extent to which we can
invoke subjectivity and objectivity and what

00:26:10.769 --> 00:26:15.960
the relation you know Amartya Sen has this
wonderful concept that he calls positional

00:26:15.960 --> 00:26:21.320
objectivity, meaning that and what I think
it means is that obviously there is no such

00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:23.420
thing as a view from nowhere.

00:26:23.420 --> 00:26:26.110
Right there is no such thing as perfect objectivity.

00:26:26.110 --> 00:26:30.700
But the impossibility of that perspective,
whatever it would be like it would, it's even

00:26:30.700 --> 00:26:34.669
impossible for us to imagine, to conceive
of what it would be like.

00:26:34.669 --> 00:26:43.120
That does not condemn us to imprisonment within
subjectivities either scientifically or humanistically

00:26:43.120 --> 00:26:44.120
conceived.

00:26:44.120 --> 00:26:51.190
That there is, there is some negotiation between
what comes from inside and what is outside

00:26:51.190 --> 00:26:57.240
that allows us to make certain claims about
our representations of things outside to say

00:26:57.240 --> 00:26:59.720
that they are accurate or true or deep etc.,
etc.

00:26:59.720 --> 00:27:00.720
So what is that?

00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:06.509
Can I ask you to drill down a little bit,
because I love the idea that you describe

00:27:06.509 --> 00:27:09.929
of the distinction between reason and causes.

00:27:09.929 --> 00:27:12.270
And it, and it feels right.

00:27:12.270 --> 00:27:18.899
I mean all of us feel like we make these choices
and it's more than just the causal influence

00:27:18.899 --> 00:27:21.259
of things that are happening inside us.

00:27:21.259 --> 00:27:23.210
There's something else.

00:27:23.210 --> 00:27:25.470
But is that real or is that an illusion?

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:29.860
Is that just the right kind of language in
order to capture a human experience?

00:27:29.860 --> 00:27:37.789
I mean, I'm interested in both of your perspectives
on this, but is there anything more beyond

00:27:37.789 --> 00:27:42.049
the physical, when we're talking about what's
happening up here?

00:27:42.049 --> 00:27:47.780
Or is it just the physical and ultimately
does it all come down to chemistry and biology

00:27:47.780 --> 00:27:50.460
and physics, or is it more than that?

00:27:50.460 --> 00:27:54.860
Now there are people who spend their lives
struggling with the so-called hard question

00:27:54.860 --> 00:27:55.860
of consciousness, right?

00:27:55.860 --> 00:27:59.899
I mean, the easy question that most people
describe is: look we can understand how the

00:27:59.899 --> 00:28:06.769
brain processes information in the world,
takes it in, and is able to generate behaviors

00:28:06.769 --> 00:28:09.059
from those stimuli.

00:28:09.059 --> 00:28:14.860
But the hard part is usually to assess what
it means to feel, what it means to actually

00:28:14.860 --> 00:28:18.899
sense what's happening out there in the world.

00:28:18.899 --> 00:28:24.990
And I, at one point, and still am to some
degree was a complete reductionist.

00:28:24.990 --> 00:28:25.990
Right?

00:28:25.990 --> 00:28:31.389
It is just the molecules and atoms and the
one thing that slightly made me question that,

00:28:31.389 --> 00:28:35.149
and I still do at some level, is, you know,
these things, was Colbert well Colbert did

00:28:35.149 --> 00:28:36.149
it too.

00:28:36.149 --> 00:28:37.149
He always does.

00:28:37.149 --> 00:28:41.440
But, but there's this old neuroscientist story
about Mary in the room.

00:28:41.440 --> 00:28:42.600
You know the one that I'm talking about?

00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:45.409
Now we have like, I don't know if everyone's
familiar with this.

00:28:45.409 --> 00:28:49.100
I don't know if it will be that useful, but
for me it was a real kicker in the head when

00:28:49.100 --> 00:28:54.279
I first encountered this and we have a little,
little video clip to describe this scenario.

00:28:54.279 --> 00:28:59.580
So if you can bring up that that Mary's room
video that would be great.

00:28:59.580 --> 00:29:06.049
Imagine that in the far, far future, there's
a brilliant neuroscientist named Mary, who

00:29:06.049 --> 00:29:12.620
for some reason is confined to a room in which
everything appears in black and white.

00:29:12.620 --> 00:29:16.580
There is no color of any sort whatsoever.

00:29:16.580 --> 00:29:23.049
Mary can study and access and examine the
world outside but it all comes to her only

00:29:23.049 --> 00:29:26.650
in black and white.

00:29:26.650 --> 00:29:31.360
Even so, Mary is able to reach a goal that
has long eluded humankind.

00:29:31.360 --> 00:29:38.049
She totally and fully unravels every last
detail about the structure of function, physiology,

00:29:38.049 --> 00:29:41.490
chemistry, biology, and physics of the brain.

00:29:41.490 --> 00:29:46.960
She knows absolutely everything there is to
know about the behavior of the brain's every

00:29:46.960 --> 00:29:49.230
neuron, every molecule, every atom.

00:29:49.230 --> 00:29:54.039
She knows precisely what goes on inside our
heads, the details of all neural processes

00:29:54.039 --> 00:30:03.539
that cascade when we see a beautiful red rose,
when we marvel at a rich blue sky, or when

00:30:03.539 --> 00:30:08.960
we encounter something in a curious shade
of art.

00:30:08.960 --> 00:30:17.019
One day, Mary is allowed to leave her room
and the very first thing she sees is a plump

00:30:17.019 --> 00:30:18.720
red tomato.

00:30:18.720 --> 00:30:27.039
Here's the question: from this experience
of the color red, will Mary learn anything

00:30:27.039 --> 00:30:28.039
new?

00:30:28.039 --> 00:30:30.110
Will she shrug and just move on?

00:30:30.110 --> 00:30:35.649
Or will she be surprised, or thrilled, or
moved, or gain some new insight through this

00:30:35.649 --> 00:30:38.260
actual experience of color?

00:30:38.260 --> 00:30:45.889
And if she does what does that tell us about
the limits of a purely physical description

00:30:45.889 --> 00:30:49.499
of the brain and consciousness?

00:30:49.499 --> 00:30:54.960
Of course, I mean the point of that story,
which was raised I guess in nineteen eighty

00:30:54.960 --> 00:30:57.820
five I think it was, Frank Jackson neuroscientist.

00:30:57.820 --> 00:31:03.399
I mean the idea of philosophy, the idea was,
if she does learn something new by virtue

00:31:03.399 --> 00:31:06.700
of actually experiencing color, or it could
be anything.

00:31:06.700 --> 00:31:07.700
It could be love.

00:31:07.700 --> 00:31:08.700
It could be passion.

00:31:08.700 --> 00:31:09.700
It could be anything.

00:31:09.700 --> 00:31:14.570
If the act of experiencing something tells
you more than just what you learned by understanding

00:31:14.570 --> 00:31:19.789
the, the physiology of it, then there's got
to be something more in the world than just

00:31:19.789 --> 00:31:20.980
the processes.

00:31:20.980 --> 00:31:26.299
I mean, is that, did that ever kick your head
the way it did mine when I first encountered

00:31:26.299 --> 00:31:27.299
it?

00:31:27.299 --> 00:31:31.140
Well I can describe to you in a recent experiment
that goes into this line.

00:31:31.140 --> 00:31:37.649
We did in our lab we got rats, adult rats
and we put infrared sensors implant in their

00:31:37.649 --> 00:31:38.789
heads.

00:31:38.789 --> 00:31:43.019
And as you know, mammals can not detect, they
cannot see infrared light because they don't

00:31:43.019 --> 00:31:44.820
have photoreceptors for their wavelength.

00:31:44.820 --> 00:31:51.770
And they don't have temperature sensors that
get, you know, a temperature sensing for infrared.

00:31:51.770 --> 00:31:56.340
And so we implanted those and we basically
put animals in the infrared universe where

00:31:56.340 --> 00:31:59.809
they have to track infrared beams to find
sugar water.

00:31:59.809 --> 00:32:02.070
And they love to go after sugar water.

00:32:02.070 --> 00:32:06.519
But what we did was to get the output of these
infrared detector electrical output that describes

00:32:06.519 --> 00:32:12.450
the magnitude and the orientation of the beam,
directly into the touch cortex the part of

00:32:12.450 --> 00:32:14.390
the brain that codes for touching.

00:32:14.390 --> 00:32:23.890
And in about three days, adult rats that have
never experienced infrared were touching otherwise

00:32:23.890 --> 00:32:24.890
invisible light.

00:32:24.890 --> 00:32:32.470
They were actually tracking beams of infrared
in finding sugar water just by experiencing,

00:32:32.470 --> 00:32:36.970
what we don't know because we still cannot
interrogate them, but they were having some

00:32:36.970 --> 00:32:43.159
sort of tactile experience and they were able
to track that infrared light.

00:32:43.159 --> 00:32:46.149
So next time, we put them in the visual cortex.

00:32:46.149 --> 00:32:51.249
Because after all, visual cortex is used to
detect electromagnetic radiation.

00:32:51.249 --> 00:32:58.350
And in six hours, these rats were able to
track infrared light with one detail.

00:32:58.350 --> 00:33:03.970
They could merge infrared wavelength with
visible spectrum.

00:33:03.970 --> 00:33:09.749
So we had a task in which half of the message
was coming visible light and half in infrared.

00:33:09.749 --> 00:33:15.840
And to solve the maze and find the sugar water,
they had to combine both of them, and they

00:33:15.840 --> 00:33:16.840
did.

00:33:16.840 --> 00:33:21.259
So and these are adult rats, adult brains
that according to many years, although the

00:33:21.259 --> 00:33:26.720
brain is plastic throughout life that piece
of cortex would be dedicated just to visible

00:33:26.720 --> 00:33:28.570
spectrum or touch.

00:33:28.570 --> 00:33:29.850
And that's what I was trying to say.

00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:35.509
I was just trying to say that the old vision
that they brain is just a passive decoder

00:33:35.509 --> 00:33:37.149
of what is there, I think is gone.

00:33:37.149 --> 00:33:43.679
But that's still describing a functional response
to a stimulus that a third party has access

00:33:43.679 --> 00:33:44.779
to, isn't it?

00:33:44.779 --> 00:33:50.909
No, because the cells in the brain when we
look at individual neurons, they now had responses

00:33:50.909 --> 00:33:55.269
to both tactile and infrared in a very nonlinear
way.

00:33:55.269 --> 00:33:59.440
We couldn't predict how these neurons would
map that relationship.

00:33:59.440 --> 00:34:06.190
So they create an internal representation
in which it was not predicted by the models

00:34:06.190 --> 00:34:10.169
that, the linear models that we're using to
try to predict, that and the animal could

00:34:10.169 --> 00:34:11.260
make sense of that.

00:34:11.260 --> 00:34:13.389
But it's all still physics and physiology.

00:34:13.389 --> 00:34:15.030
There's nothing more than that, right?

00:34:15.030 --> 00:34:16.030
No.

00:34:16.030 --> 00:34:17.030
Absolutely.

00:34:17.030 --> 00:34:18.030
But that's the reason I'm saying .
But is that your view?

00:34:18.030 --> 00:34:22.850
No, no, I'm just trying to say that the reason
the brain is so complex.

00:34:22.850 --> 00:34:27.420
And although the laws of physics of course
have the basis of its functionally is there

00:34:27.420 --> 00:34:30.140
is a self adapting complex system.

00:34:30.140 --> 00:34:36.820
It's like an orchestra that when it plays
a song, at the very moment the note is produced

00:34:36.820 --> 00:34:40.450
it's already changing the instruments to produce
the next note.

00:34:40.450 --> 00:34:43.780
And this is a recursive loop that goes continuously.

00:34:43.780 --> 00:34:46.810
But there are many ways to play a song.

00:34:46.810 --> 00:34:48.420
There are many ways to solve.

