00:51:41.500 --> 00:51:47.110
In my opinion, is building its own internal
point of view as we grow up, and as reason

00:51:47.110 --> 00:51:52.640
humans have so long of a time of postnatal
development, and why we play so much.

00:51:52.640 --> 00:51:56.820
We are playing scenarios, we are practicing
scenarios, and we are building an account

00:51:56.820 --> 00:51:57.970
of what is out here.

00:51:57.970 --> 00:52:05.320
And in my conception of the brain, every time
I'm about to look to something, I'm actually

00:52:05.320 --> 00:52:08.460
seeing before I look, because my brain has
a hypothesis.

00:52:08.460 --> 00:52:12.370
He has, he has an expectation of what I'm
about to see.

00:52:12.370 --> 00:52:18.770
And it's this conclusion, or this collision,
I'm sorry, of what is here with this expectation

00:52:18.770 --> 00:52:21.980
Yeah, it's a kind of scientists Kantianism,
or some kind.

00:52:21.980 --> 00:52:26.250
Meaning that we dont- there is no such thing
as naked sense experience.

00:52:26.250 --> 00:52:31.380
We -actually bring organizing concepts for,
in other words, we don't look there and see

00:52:31.380 --> 00:52:36.530
seven brown lines is raw sense data and then
look at combining them into a chair.

00:52:36.530 --> 00:52:38.120
We see a chair.

00:52:38.120 --> 00:52:42.670
And then we begin to break it down into what
the sensory data are that allowed us to see

00:52:42.670 --> 00:52:43.670
that.

00:52:43.670 --> 00:52:48.550
But I think this is actually why when you
say beauty, when I say beauty or happiness,

00:52:48.550 --> 00:52:50.640
when I see something as beautiful.

00:52:50.640 --> 00:52:57.400
It's very difficult to define, because my
internal definition of beautiful carries my

00:52:57.400 --> 00:53:04.580
entire lifetime story, my evolutionary story
in the, in the salt and pepper of my previous

00:53:04.580 --> 00:53:05.960
experiences, which are very different from
yours.

00:53:05.960 --> 00:53:07.230
Which are very different from his.

00:53:07.230 --> 00:53:12.300
Can raise here a related question, which is
the degree to which, you know, the title of

00:53:12.300 --> 00:53:14.530
the program is "To Unweave a Rainbow," right?

00:53:14.530 --> 00:53:20.580
It comes from a Keats poem, this notion that
science somehow pulls things apart and destroys

00:53:20.580 --> 00:53:22.780
the beauty and the wonder of it all.

00:53:22.780 --> 00:53:27.860
And you know Richard Feynman had a perspective,
a great physicist, had a perspective on this,

00:53:27.860 --> 00:53:33.690
just want to get your sense of it, can you
show the Richard Feynman flower clip?

00:53:33.690 --> 00:53:39.300
I have a friend who is an artist and he has
sometimes taken a view, which I don't agree

00:53:39.300 --> 00:53:40.300
with very well.

00:53:40.300 --> 00:53:43.440
You hold up a flower and say, "Look how beautiful
it is."

00:53:43.440 --> 00:53:46.740
And he says, "You see his eyes and can see
how beautiful this is.

00:53:46.740 --> 00:53:54.430
But you as a scientist, take this all apart
and it becomes a dull thing."

00:53:54.430 --> 00:53:57.280
And I think that he's kind of nutty.

00:53:57.280 --> 00:53:58.480
First of all, although I may not be quite
as refined and aesthetic as he is, but I can

00:53:58.480 --> 00:54:00.540
appreciate the beauty of a flower.

00:54:00.540 --> 00:54:04.010
At the same time, I see much more about the
flower than he sees.

00:54:04.010 --> 00:54:07.150
I mean, it's not just beauty at this dimension,
there's also beauty at a smaller dimension.

00:54:07.150 --> 00:54:09.820
I could imagine cells in there.

00:54:09.820 --> 00:54:13.900
Also the processes, the complicated actions,
which also have a beauty.

00:54:13.900 --> 00:54:18.730
The fact that the colors in the flower evolve
in order to attract insects upon him, is interesting.

00:54:18.730 --> 00:54:22.070
It means the insects can see the color.

00:54:22.070 --> 00:54:23.070
It heads a question.

00:54:23.070 --> 00:54:24.150
Why is it aesthetic?

00:54:24.150 --> 00:54:28.860
All kinds of interesting questions which science
only adds to the excitement, the mystery,

00:54:28.860 --> 00:54:30.570
and the awe of a flower.

00:54:30.570 --> 00:54:31.570
It only adds.

00:54:31.570 --> 00:54:38.000
I don't understand how it subtracts.

00:54:38.000 --> 00:54:40.810
Thoughts?

00:54:40.810 --> 00:54:48.900
Well I look, obviously-well in the first place,
there's no question that science itself and

00:54:48.900 --> 00:54:57.630
its discoveries have been one of the greatest
revealers of beauty that man has ever experienced

00:54:57.630 --> 00:54:59.690
or devised.

00:54:59.690 --> 00:55:04.790
And that's why there are many scientific results
having to do both with science in the small

00:55:04.790 --> 00:55:10.730
and the cell and then you in the galaxy, before
which one feels not just the feeling of being

00:55:10.730 --> 00:55:14.420
present,, being present before beauty but
even before the sublime.

00:55:14.420 --> 00:55:15.420
Yes.

00:55:15.420 --> 00:55:17.240
Which is another form of aesthetic experience.

00:55:17.240 --> 00:55:22.790
What I would say about, what about Feynman,
is that the whole thing begs the question,

00:55:22.790 --> 00:55:27.260
which is to say that the point is not that
scientists-

00:55:27.260 --> 00:55:32.480
The good news is that science is powerless
to destroy beauty even if it thinks it can.

00:55:32.480 --> 00:55:33.790
That's the good news.

00:55:33.790 --> 00:55:38.590
So all these physicalists running around thinking
they're, they're murdering to dissect they're

00:55:38.590 --> 00:55:39.590
wrong.

00:55:39.590 --> 00:55:43.880
They're dissecting, but they're not murdering,
except for themselves, except for themselves.

00:55:43.880 --> 00:55:45.420
And a pity on them.

00:55:45.420 --> 00:55:50.770
But, but Feynman begs the question in the
sense that when he says you can experience

00:55:50.770 --> 00:55:55.160
the beauty not just at the flower but also
at the cellular level, I would say all right,

00:55:55.160 --> 00:56:00.420
but there still remains the question of what
that experience of beauty is at any level,

00:56:00.420 --> 00:56:01.790
at any level.

00:56:01.790 --> 00:56:09.920
So, and the idea that the experience of beauty
can be explained scientifically at any level

00:56:09.920 --> 00:56:18.510
of scientific revelation, seems imperialistic
to me and seems incorrect to me.

00:56:18.510 --> 00:56:24.401
So the awe that, I mean, his instruction is:
don't think that it's just at the level of

00:56:24.401 --> 00:56:25.401
the flower.

00:56:25.401 --> 00:56:26.760
We scientists can see it too.

00:56:26.760 --> 00:56:30.790
And I say yeah, but then I say to him, "Well
what exactly do you think you're experiencing?"

00:56:30.790 --> 00:56:35.800
And if science could give a real, it can't
yet, but if it really could get an answer.

00:56:35.800 --> 00:56:36.800
It never will.

00:56:36.800 --> 00:56:38.680
Well just go with me for half a moment.

00:56:38.680 --> 00:56:43.980
If it could give an answer to that would it,
what impact would that have on your perspective?

00:56:43.980 --> 00:56:46.081
Let's say, let's just say, would it matter
to you?

00:56:46.081 --> 00:56:49.780
Would you say, "Oh goodness gracious, I can
no longer think about the world as I once

00:56:49.780 --> 00:56:51.830
did," or would your view just sort of sit
on top-

00:56:51.830 --> 00:56:55.060
In terms of my own experience, it wouldn't
matter one whit.

00:56:55.060 --> 00:57:01.300
What would matter to me is that I don't want
my fellow citizens to believe that they are

00:57:01.300 --> 00:57:10.620
nothing but manipulatable physical beings
who, who lack- I don't want, I don't want

00:57:10.620 --> 00:57:16.730
their horizon for- the possible horizon of
their experience.

00:57:16.730 --> 00:57:24.040
I don't want their sense of what, of what
their spiritual attainments can be to be shrunk

00:57:24.040 --> 00:57:26.710
by the idea that it's all just matter.

00:57:26.710 --> 00:57:27.940
but does it have to be shrunk?

00:57:27.940 --> 00:57:30.240
Well, generally it's had that effect.

00:57:30.240 --> 00:57:32.540
But it doesn't need to
No, it doesn't need to at all.

00:57:32.540 --> 00:57:33.540
Not at all.

00:57:33.540 --> 00:57:35.240
I think, I think the two views can find a
common ground because

00:57:35.240 --> 00:57:36.960
Not at all.

00:57:36.960 --> 00:57:43.360
I think the definitions of beauty that we
saw with Feynman's description is in the eye

00:57:43.360 --> 00:57:45.390
of the beholder, right?

00:57:45.390 --> 00:57:51.530
You can-what I think-my criticism of the reductionist
view, as a scientist, is that yes there is

00:57:51.530 --> 00:57:56.560
beauty and there's always an adventure in
the intellectual journey of breaking things

00:57:56.560 --> 00:58:00.930
apart and trying to fix them, because physics
was so successful.

00:58:00.930 --> 00:58:02.320
Physics was so successful.

00:58:02.320 --> 00:58:07.250
Everybody thought, OK the recipe for success
in other disciplines is just to get that model

00:58:07.250 --> 00:58:10.570
and try to apply it to, to biology and other
things.

00:58:10.570 --> 00:58:13.800
And the problem is, is pretty much what you're
saying.

00:58:13.800 --> 00:58:15.470
We are breaking apart.

00:58:15.470 --> 00:58:18.910
We try to find the fundamental elements in
biology is very difficult, because when you

00:58:18.910 --> 00:58:21.380
say "life," it is not like when you say "matter."

00:58:21.380 --> 00:58:22.380
Yes.

00:58:22.380 --> 00:58:26.520
When you say "matter," you say is made of
molecules, is made of atoms, is made of the

00:58:26.520 --> 00:58:28.830
nucleus, and quarks, and strings.

00:58:28.830 --> 00:58:35.380
When you say "life," for biologists it's not
very easy to, to come with this infinite reductionist

00:58:35.380 --> 00:58:36.880
view of what it is.

00:58:36.880 --> 00:58:42.860
So our problem is that the science of complexity
in biology is at its infancy.

00:58:42.860 --> 00:58:48.340
We really can't- we have very few ideas and
tools to put things together once we break

00:58:48.340 --> 00:58:49.470
them apart.

00:58:49.470 --> 00:58:54.590
But then do you think that the question of
what complexity means and how it should be

00:58:54.590 --> 00:59:01.900
interpreted would be changed or in any way
affected by the discovery of unimaginable

00:59:01.900 --> 00:59:03.180
degrees of complexity?

00:59:03.180 --> 00:59:09.390
In other words, does it really matter that
the complexity is going to get greater in-for

00:59:09.390 --> 00:59:12.650
the search for what the meanings of that complexity
will be?

00:59:12.650 --> 00:59:18.960
Yeah I never thought about it, but what I
can tell you is that the way we approach a

00:59:18.960 --> 00:59:26.480
complex system today like the brain, is with
tools that we approach systems that are not

00:59:26.480 --> 00:59:29.910
self adaptive, self-referral, in as complex
as this system is.

00:59:29.910 --> 00:59:34.270
Because when you're talk about a functioning
brain, you are talking about an exchange of

00:59:34.270 --> 00:59:39.420
information from multiple levels, from the
circuit level, from the cellular level, from

00:59:39.420 --> 00:59:41.240
the molecule, from the quantum level.

00:59:41.240 --> 00:59:47.230
And you're also talking about a descriptive
system, like Prigogine used to say, with environment.

00:59:47.230 --> 00:59:50.620
You're exchanging information, exchanging
energy, materials.

00:59:50.620 --> 00:59:51.670
Right?

00:59:51.670 --> 00:59:56.720
You have an island of negative entropy trying
to, to keep together, not to dissipate.

00:59:56.720 --> 01:00:03.580
And the tools that we have to approach these
systems are, in my opinion, at their infancy,

01:00:03.580 --> 01:00:05.360
and that's the problem.

01:00:05.360 --> 01:00:06.820
You can break it apart.

01:00:06.820 --> 01:00:11.330
And in physics it works very nicely, but in
biology we should be able to put it together.

01:00:11.330 --> 01:00:13.520
And that's where the difficulty emerges.

01:00:13.520 --> 01:00:17.230
But I think we're sort of both, from different
ways, arriving in a similar place.

01:00:17.230 --> 01:00:19.660
It reminds me of, do you know the parable?

01:00:19.660 --> 01:00:23.680
There was a British philosopher named John
Wisdom who once gave a parable, created a,

01:00:23.680 --> 01:00:28.960
made up a parable about the question of whether
or not-of what's called the Cosmological Proof

01:00:28.960 --> 01:00:33.670
of God's Existence, the argument for design,
that owing to the complexity that we see in

01:00:33.670 --> 01:00:35.590
nature, there must be a designer.

01:00:35.590 --> 01:00:40.350
So Wisdom made up the following parable: There
were two explorers in a jungle, let's say

01:00:40.350 --> 01:00:44.720
the Amazon jungle, and they're cutting away
and cutting away and cutting away and they

01:00:44.720 --> 01:00:48.730
enter deeper and deeper into the jungle and
suddenly, they come upon an acre of perfectly

01:00:48.730 --> 01:00:53.110
manicured and landscaped lawn, a beautifully
kept garden.

01:00:53.110 --> 01:00:56.600
So one of them says, "It's chance."

01:00:56.600 --> 01:00:58.700
The other says, "No, no, no, there's a gardener."

01:00:58.700 --> 01:01:01.420
So the one who says it's chance says, "You
know, why don't we stay the night and see

01:01:01.420 --> 01:01:03.100
if a gardener turns up."

01:01:03.100 --> 01:01:04.100
So they stay the night.

01:01:04.100 --> 01:01:05.970
Of course, no gardener turns up.

01:01:05.970 --> 01:01:08.220
So the man says, "I told you there's no gardener."

01:01:08.220 --> 01:01:10.950
So the one who believes there's a gardener
say "No, no, no, all that means is that maybe

01:01:10.950 --> 01:01:12.510
we couldn't see him at night.

01:01:12.510 --> 01:01:14.340
Okay we'll rig up some lights."

01:01:14.340 --> 01:01:15.340
They stay.

01:01:15.340 --> 01:01:16.340
Nobody comes.

01:01:16.340 --> 01:01:17.340
"You see I told you, it was chance."

01:01:17.340 --> 01:01:21.000
He says, "No, no, no, it's just a gardener
that can't be seen at night in a light.

01:01:21.000 --> 01:01:22.810
Then let's put up an electric force field."

01:01:22.810 --> 01:01:24.610
They put up an electric force field.

01:01:24.610 --> 01:01:25.610
Nobody comes.

01:01:25.610 --> 01:01:27.230
"You see I told you, it's chance."

01:01:27.230 --> 01:01:30.281
He says, "No, no, all we know is that the
gardener is invisible at night in the light

01:01:30.281 --> 01:01:32.290
and immune to the electric force field."

01:01:32.290 --> 01:01:35.991
They do this on and on until the first one
says to the second, "You know what, let's

01:01:35.991 --> 01:01:39.770
go because what you mean by a gardener, I
mean by chance."

01:01:39.770 --> 01:01:42.660
You just described a Swiss garden.

01:01:42.660 --> 01:01:43.660
Yes, exactly.

01:01:43.660 --> 01:01:45.620
Because I have been there for three years.

01:01:45.620 --> 01:01:51.590
They look pristine and I've never seen the
gardener ever.

01:01:51.590 --> 01:01:58.120
So I got a question from you from our streaming
audience: Do you think there's moral worth

01:01:58.120 --> 01:02:01.580
to taking one position that you're describing
over another?

01:02:01.580 --> 01:02:05.690
Why might it be valuable to accept the limits
of science?

01:02:05.690 --> 01:02:10.710
Well look, we haven't talked about the moral
implications of these things, and the reason

01:02:10.710 --> 01:02:14.580
I didn't bring them up is because the first
thing that matters is whether or not what

01:02:14.580 --> 01:02:18.880
we're saying is true, not whether we're saying
it's morally useful or socially-that it has

01:02:18.880 --> 01:02:20.810
any kind of social utility.

01:02:20.810 --> 01:02:25.960
I think obviously there are different scientific
accounts of human life that have different

01:02:25.960 --> 01:02:28.510
ethical implications.

01:02:28.510 --> 01:02:32.640
You know, you just look at the history of
the moral interpretation of Darwinism, for

01:02:32.640 --> 01:02:33.640
example.

01:02:33.640 --> 01:02:38.360
In the late 19th century, Darwinism was interpreted
in an almost fascist way, the survival of

01:02:38.360 --> 01:02:39.900
the fittest and so on.

01:02:39.900 --> 01:02:47.280
But then liberal evolutionary biologist, 20
and 30 years ago, began to argue that actually

01:02:47.280 --> 01:02:49.680
natural selection is of course the case.

01:02:49.680 --> 01:02:53.030
But what we're naturally selected for is not
cruelty but altruism.

01:02:53.030 --> 01:02:58.620
And so you have a whole literature about the
evolutionary origins of altruism which accepts

01:02:58.620 --> 01:03:04.540
the biological evolutionary determinism at
some level but has a totally different moral

01:03:04.540 --> 01:03:05.620
implication.

01:03:05.620 --> 01:03:11.520
The real question is not-the first question
has to be not what are the different moral

01:03:11.520 --> 01:03:14.900
implications of different scientific pictures
of life.

01:03:14.900 --> 01:03:19.260
The real question is: does morality need a
basis in science at all?

01:03:19.260 --> 01:03:20.610
Right.

01:03:20.610 --> 01:03:27.840
You know it is, are ethics based- are ethics
naturalistic or are they not?

01:03:27.840 --> 01:03:30.190
And there are obviously different views about
this.

01:03:30.190 --> 01:03:34.960
Well the traditional view, of course, is that
science doesn't have values and it's just

01:03:34.960 --> 01:03:38.010
Well, but if this is-
but that's a very simplistic way of looking

01:03:38.010 --> 01:03:39.100
at things, of course.

01:03:39.100 --> 01:03:40.100
Yes.

01:03:40.100 --> 01:03:41.100
Yes.

01:03:41.100 --> 01:03:44.540
But then there's a very interesting debate,
because look at today what is happening.

01:03:44.540 --> 01:03:49.990
If you read any known regular book on robotics,
what is robotics and artificial intelligence

01:03:49.990 --> 01:03:56.740
is doing out there in the economies, around
the world, in how many-you know in the next

01:03:56.740 --> 01:04:03.290
decades or so, how robotics may displace millions
of jobs because menial jobs, or not so menial

01:04:03.290 --> 01:04:06.530
jobs, may be done by, by machines.

01:04:06.530 --> 01:04:12.240
So when I read this literature and I read
descriptions that try to compare us to machines

01:04:12.240 --> 01:04:18.630
to digital computers, there I see a devaluation
